  "$schema": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/opena2a-standards/atx-conformance/main/schemas/fixture-v1.schema.json",
  "name": "atx-v1_1/declared-purpose-integer-respelled-upper-exponent",
  "description": "An ATX v1.1 credential signed over declaredPurpose {\"n\": 1000}, carrying {\"n\": 1E3}: the same value written with an exponent part that opens with an upper-case E. declaredPurpose enters the TBS as written (atx-spec §1.3a.2 rule 5), and §1.3a.2 rule 3 keeps RFC 8785 number serialization out of cross-language scope, so no signature covers a number there written with a fraction part or an exponent part. Its form is decided from the credential's JSON text, not from the value a JSON parser returns for it. The verifier MUST REJECT as SIGNATURE_INVALID with a reason that names declaredPurpose. An RFC 8785 serializer that reads each number as an IEEE 754 double writes 1E3 as 1000, the bytes the signature covers, so a verifier that canonicalizes the number that way, or that finds an exponent part by a lower-case e alone, verifies the signature and wrongly ACCEPTs.",
  "requirement": "§1.3a.2 rule 3: RFC 8785 number serialization is kept out of cross-language scope, so a declaredPurpose number written with an exponent part that opens with an upper-case E, 1E3 where the signature covers 1000, has no bytes the signature covers and is a signature failure",
